32
00:08:22,994 --> 00:08:55,658
Protocol and design detail: logic (4-State, Single Driver) (1). logic: Replaces wire and reg when there is a single driver; can be used in both continuous and procedural assignments. Rule: A logic variable must have exactly one driver (one assign, or one always block, or one port connection). 4-state: 0, 1, X, Z (same as wire/reg). Ports: input logic, output logic; no need to choose wire vs...

33
00:08:56,008 --> 00:09:13,864
Protocol and design detail: logic (4-State, Single Driver) (2). Internal: Use logic for single-driver signals; use wire only when needed (e.g. multiple drivers, tri-state). From MODULE4 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

34
00:09:14,214 --> 00:09:46,758
Protocol and design detail: bit and 2-State Types (1800). bit: 2-state (0, 1); no X or Z. Often used in testbenches and for indices. int, byte, shortint, longint: Integer types; 2-state unless declared with a 4-state base. Use in RTL: Optional (e.g. loop indices, parameters); many RTL designs use logic and integer/reg for compatibility. From MODULE4 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

35
00:09:47,108 --> 00:10:17,396
Protocol and design detail: always_comb. Intent: Combinational logic; no storage; outputs are a function of inputs only. Sensitivity: Automatically inferred (like always @*); must not contain latches. Tool check: Simulator/synthesis can warn if the block implies storage or is sensitive to clock edges. Prefer over always @* when the tool supports it: clearer intent and better checking. From...

36
00:10:17,746 --> 00:10:45,994
Protocol and design detail: always_ff. Intent: Sequential logic (flip-flops); use nonblocking assignment (<=) only. Sensitivity: Must list exactly one clock (and optionally async reset); no other event controls. Tool check: Can warn if blocking assignment or incomplete sensitivity is used. From MODULE4 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

37
00:10:46,344 --> 00:11:19,080
Protocol and design detail: always_latch. Intent: Explicit latch (level-sensitive storage). Use sparingly; usually latches are unintended. When: When a latch is truly intended (e.g. transparent latch); otherwise use always_comb or always_ff. Recommendation: Prefer always_comb or always_ff; use always_latch only when a latch is desired. From MODULE4 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

38
00:11:19,430 --> 00:11:42,182
Protocol and design detail: Interface Declaration. Signals: Declared once inside the interface; shared by all modules that use it. modport: Defines a view (direction) for a given role (e.g. master sees data as output, slave sees data as input). From MODULE4 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

39
00:11:42,532 --> 00:12:02,980
Protocol and design detail: Using an Interface in a Module. Port: Single interface port with a modport (e.g. bus_if.master bus). Access: bus.signal_name; direction is enforced by the modport. From MODULE4 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

40
00:12:03,330 --> 00:12:17,058
Protocol and design detail: Top-Level Connection. One interface instance: Connects master and slave; no long port lists. From MODULE4 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

41
00:12:17,408 --> 00:12:38,120
Protocol and design detail: Package Declaration. Contents: parameter, localparam, typedef, function, task, etc. (no always blocks). Scope: Items are in namespace pkg_util:: until imported. From MODULE4 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

42
00:12:38,470 --> 00:13:00,118
Protocol and design detail: Import and Use. import pkg_util::*: Import all names from the package (can cause name clashes). import pkg_util::item: Import specific items; safer when many packages are used. From MODULE4 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

43
00:13:00,468 --> 00:13:28,716
Protocol and design detail: unique case. Semantics: At most one case item must match at runtime; if none or more than one matches, a runtime error can be reported. Use: When the design guarantees at most one match (e.g. one-hot encoding, mutually exclusive conditions). Synthesis: Can be implemented as parallel priority logic; no inferred priority order. From MODULE4 Topics Covered —...

